Subject: turn journal checksums on at format time

lwext4's mkfs writes a bare JBD superblock: no feature flags, no checksum type,
no uuid. The journal it lays down is the 1998 format, so a torn transaction is
indistinguishable from a complete one and recovery replays whatever is in the
log.

Set JBD_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_CSUM_V3 and crc32c, which is what mke2fs has done by
default for years. The checksums are seeded with the journal superblock's uuid,
so it has to carry the filesystem's -- copying it is not cosmetic, and without
it every checksum computes against zeroes and Linux rejects the journal.

Depends on 0015: before that fix the commit checksum was written in host byte
order, so enabling the feature made every volume unrecoverable rather than
more recoverable.

--- a/src/ext4_mkfs.c
+++ b/src/ext4_mkfs.c
@@ -48,6 +48,7 @@
 #include <ext4_fs.h>
 #include <ext4_inode.h>
 #include <ext4_ialloc.h>
+#include <ext4_crc32.h>
 #include <ext4_mkfs.h>
 
 #include <inttypes.h>
@@ -752,6 +753,24 @@
 		jbd_sb->first = to_be32(1);
 		jbd_sb->sequence = to_be32(1);
 
+		/* Checksum the journal, as mke2fs does. Without it a torn
+		 * transaction is indistinguishable from a complete one, so
+		 * recovery replays whatever is in the log over a live
+		 * filesystem. With it, replay stops at the first commit block
+		 * that does not verify: the change is lost, which is what a
+		 * crash means, rather than the volume being corrupted, which is
+		 * not. That is the only defence against a drive that reports a
+		 * cache flush and does not perform one, which no barrier can
+		 * help with.
+		 *
+		 * The checksums are seeded with the journal superblock's own
+		 * uuid, so it has to carry the filesystem's. */
+		jbd_sb->feature_incompat = to_be32(JBD_FEATURE_INCOMPAT_CSUM_V3);
+		jbd_sb->checksum_type = JBD_CRC32C_CHKSUM;
+		memcpy(jbd_sb->uuid, fs->sb.uuid, sizeof(jbd_sb->uuid));
+		jbd_sb->checksum = to_be32(ext4_crc32c(EXT4_CRC32_INIT, jbd_sb,
+						       JBD_SUPERBLOCK_SIZE));
+
 		ext4_bcache_set_dirty(blk.buf);
 		ret = ext4_block_set(fs->bdev, &blk);
 		if (ret != EOK)
